export default {
id:'science-h-ap-psych-biological-bases-of-behavior',subject:'science',level:5,grade:9,standard:'AP Psychology Course and Exam Description (2024-25 onward), Unit 1, topics 1.1–1.6',title:'Biological Bases of Behavior',
why:'Explain how genes, neurons, hormones and brain structures produce behavior, how sleep and consciousness work, and how the senses turn energy into neural signals, with evidence a psychologist would accept.',
skills:{heredity:'Interaction of heredity and environment',nervous:'Overview of the nervous system',neuron:'The neuron and neural firing',brain:'The brain',sleep:'Sleep',sensation:'Sensation'},
outline:[['1.1','Interaction of Heredity and Environment',['heredity']],['1.2','Overview of the Nervous System',['nervous']],['1.3','The Neuron and Neural Firing',['neuron']],['1.4','The Brain',['brain']],['1.5','Sleep',['sleep']],['1.6','Sensation',['sensation']]],
steps:[
 {skill:'heredity',say:'Behavior comes from nature and nurture working together, and the two cannot be cleanly separated. Genes are segments of DNA on chromosomes, and humans typically have 23 pairs. Most psychological traits are polygenic: many genes each add a small effect. Genotype is the genetic makeup; phenotype is what can be observed, produced by genes plus environment. Epigenetic marks can change how active a gene is without changing the DNA sequence, so experience can influence gene expression. Evolutionary psychology asks how traits may have helped ancestors survive and reproduce through natural selection; its explanations are hypotheses that still need evidence.',eq:'genotype + environment (+ epigenetic effects) → phenotype',pic:GENES},
 {skill:'heredity',say:'Behavior geneticists compare relatives to estimate genetic influence. Identical (monozygotic) twins share nearly all of their segregating genes, and fraternal (dizygotic) twins share about half on average. A concordance rate is the share of pairs in which both twins have a trait, among pairs with at least one affected twin. In a fictional study, 21 identical pairs both show a trait and 14 have only one affected twin, so 21 ÷ 35 = 0.60. For fraternal pairs, 12 both and 28 one gives 12 ÷ 40 = 0.30. The higher rate suggests a genetic contribution, not a purely genetic cause. Heritability describes differences within a group, never the share of one person\'s trait that is genetic.',eq:'21 ÷ (21 + 14) = 0.60 versus 12 ÷ (12 + 28) = 0.30',pic:TWIN,check:{prompt:TRY[0],answer:.7,options:[.7,.3,.4286],hint:'Divide pairs with both twins affected by pairs with at least one twin affected, which is both plus one.',pic:bars('Fictional twin pairs',['Both affected','One affected'],[14,6])}},
 {skill:'nervous',say:'The central nervous system (CNS) is the brain and spinal cord. The peripheral nervous system (PNS) carries messages between the CNS and the rest of the body. Its somatic division brings in sensory information and controls voluntary skeletal muscles. Its autonomic division regulates organs and glands automatically: the sympathetic branch arouses the body for fight or flight, and the parasympathetic branch calms it for rest and digestion. Sensory (afferent) neurons carry information toward the CNS, motor (efferent) neurons carry commands out, and interneurons link them inside the CNS. Glial cells support neurons.',eq:'CNS + PNS; PNS = somatic + autonomic (sympathetic, parasympathetic)',pic:TREE},
 {skill:'nervous',say:'In a spinal reflex, such as pulling a hand from a hot pan, a sensory neuron sends the message to an interneuron in the spinal cord and a motor neuron signals the muscle, so the response can begin before the brain registers pain. The endocrine system works differently: glands release hormones into the bloodstream, and they act on target cells with matching receptors. Compared with neural messages, hormonal messages usually travel more slowly and last longer. The pituitary gland influences other glands, the adrenal glands release epinephrine during stress, the pancreas regulates blood sugar, the pineal gland releases melatonin, and the hypothalamus links the two systems.',eq:'nerve message: fast, brief; hormone message: slower, longer lasting',pic:REFLEX},
 {skill:'neuron',say:'A neuron receives signals through its dendrites, combines them in the cell body (soma), and sends an action potential down its axon toward the terminal buttons. Many axons are wrapped in a myelin sheath, made by glial cells, with small gaps called nodes of Ranvier. Myelin speeds conduction, and damage to it, as in multiple sclerosis, disrupts signaling. The drawing is a schematic with the lengths of the parts in rough proportion, not a photograph of a real neuron.',eq:'dendrites → soma → axon → terminal buttons',pic:NEURON},
 {skill:'neuron',say:'At rest, the inside of a neuron is negative compared with the outside, a resting potential of about -70 millivolts in typical textbook examples. Excitatory input pushes the membrane toward threshold, about -55 mV in the same examples, so it must rise by 15 mV. At threshold, sodium channels open and sodium rushes in, making the inside briefly positive; potassium leaving then restores the negative state. The action potential is all-or-none: its size does not change with stimulus strength. Intensity is coded by how often neurons fire and how many fire. A refractory period follows, when firing again is harder. Values vary among neurons and sources.',eq:'-55 - (-70) = 15 mV to reach threshold',pic:AP},
 {skill:'neuron',say:'When an action potential reaches the axon terminal, vesicles release neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft. The molecules bind to receptors on the next neuron; an excitatory effect makes it more likely to fire and an inhibitory effect makes it less likely. The presynaptic neuron then reabsorbs much of the transmitter in reuptake. Acetylcholine helps with muscle action and memory; dopamine with reward and movement, and loss of dopamine neurons occurs in Parkinson\'s disease; serotonin with mood, sleep and hunger; GABA is the main inhibitory transmitter and glutamate the main excitatory one; endorphins help reduce pain.',eq:'release → bind → excite or inhibit → reuptake',pic:SYN},
 {skill:'neuron',say:'Psychoactive drugs change neural firing. An agonist mimics a neurotransmitter or boosts its action, as opioids do at endorphin receptors. An antagonist blocks receptors so the transmitter cannot act, as curare does at acetylcholine receptors. Reuptake blockers leave more transmitter in the cleft: cocaine does this for dopamine and SSRIs for serotonin. Depressants such as alcohol slow neural activity, stimulants such as caffeine and nicotine speed it up, opioids reduce pain, and hallucinogens such as LSD alter perception. Tolerance means a larger dose is needed for the same effect; withdrawal is discomfort after use is reduced or stopped.',eq:'agonist mimics | antagonist blocks | reuptake blocker prolongs',pic:DRUGS},
 {skill:'brain',say:'In the brainstem, the medulla controls heartbeat and breathing and the pons helps with sleep and arousal; the reticular formation regulates alertness. The cerebellum coordinates voluntary movement and balance. Deep in the forebrain, the thalamus relays most sensory information to the cortex, the hypothalamus regulates hunger, thirst, body temperature and the pituitary gland, and the limbic system includes the amygdala, important for fear and emotion, and the hippocampus, important for forming new explicit memories. The drawing is a schematic, not to scale.',eq:'medulla: heartbeat, breathing | cerebellum: balance, coordination',pic:BRAINSTEM},
 {skill:'brain',say:'The cerebral cortex has four lobes. The frontal lobes support planning, judgment and voluntary movement through the motor cortex, and Broca\'s area, usually in the left frontal lobe, supports producing speech. The parietal lobes hold the somatosensory cortex for touch. The occipital lobes process vision. The temporal lobes process hearing, and Wernicke\'s area, usually in the left temporal lobe, supports understanding language. Association areas integrate information. The case of Phineas Gage suggested that frontal areas affect personality, though accounts of his change were partly anecdotal.',eq:'frontal: planning | parietal: touch | occipital: vision | temporal: hearing',pic:LOBES},
 {skill:'brain',say:'The two cerebral hemispheres are joined mainly by the corpus callosum. Language is lateralized to the left hemisphere in most people, and spatial tasks often lean on the right, yet nearly every complex task uses both, so strict left-brained or right-brained types are a myth. In split-brain patients, whose corpus callosum was cut to treat severe epilepsy, information shown to one visual field reaches only the opposite hemisphere. Neuroplasticity is the brain\'s ability to reorganize connections with experience or after injury, which helps explain partial recovery after a stroke, although recovery has limits.',eq:'right visual field → left hemisphere → can usually be named aloud',pic:SPLIT},
 {skill:'brain',say:'Researchers choose a tool for the question. An EEG records electrical activity through scalp electrodes and shows timing well. CT and MRI show structure; MRI uses magnets and radio waves and gives finer detail of soft tissue. Functional MRI tracks blood oxygen changes to show which areas are active during a task, and PET uses a radioactive tracer to show energy use. Lesion studies examine behavior after damage to an area, which suggests but does not prove that the area performs a function. Activity during a task is a correlation, not proof of a single center for a complex behavior.',eq:'structure: CT, MRI | activity: EEG, fMRI, PET',pic:TOOLS},
 {skill:'sleep',say:'A circadian rhythm lasts about 24 hours and is guided by the suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus, with light affecting melatonin. Sleep alternates between NREM stages and REM in cycles of about 90 minutes in adults. Relaxed wakefulness shows alpha waves. Stage 1 is light sleep, stage 2 adds sleep spindles and K-complexes, and stage 3 is deep sleep with slow delta waves. REM has rapid eye movements, vivid dreams, muscle paralysis and brain activity like wakefulness. Early cycles have more stage 3, and REM periods lengthen later in the night. The graph is a fictional, schematic night.',eq:'one cycle ≈ 90 min: N1 → N2 → N3 → N2 → REM',pic:NIGHT},
 {skill:'sleep',say:'Insomnia is persistent trouble falling or staying asleep, narcolepsy involves sudden attacks of sleep, and sleep apnea involves repeated pauses in breathing. Night terrors usually occur in deep NREM sleep, while nightmares occur in REM. After REM deprivation, people show REM rebound. Freud\'s wish-fulfillment theory distinguishes manifest from latent content and has limited support; activation-synthesis theory says the cortex interprets random neural activity. Sleep supports memory consolidation, and too little sleep impairs attention and mood. To count cycles, a fictional sleeper with 90-minute cycles who sleeps 8 hours has 480 ÷ 90 = 5 full cycles with 30 minutes left over.',eq:'480 min ÷ 90 min = 5 full cycles, 30 min left',pic:DISORDERS,check:{prompt:TRY[1],answer:4,options:[4,4.3,5],hint:'Change hours to minutes, then divide by 90 and count only complete cycles.',pic:CYCLE}},
 {skill:'sensation',say:'Sensation begins when receptors transduce physical energy into neural signals; perception organizes and interprets them. The absolute threshold is the minimum stimulation detected half the time, and the difference threshold, or just noticeable difference (JND), is the smallest change detected half the time. Weber\'s law says the JND is roughly a constant proportion of the original stimulus, so JND = k × standard. In a fictional experiment with k = 0.05 and a 200 g standard, the JND is 0.05 × 200 = 10 g. Signal detection theory separates hits, misses, false alarms and correct rejections: with 40 hits and 10 misses the hit rate is 40 ÷ 50 = 0.80. Sensory adaptation lowers sensitivity to constant stimulation.',eq:'JND = 0.05 × 200 g = 10 g',pic:WEBER(.05,200,'JND = 0.05 × standard'),check:{prompt:TRY[2],answer:.06,options:[.06,16.6667,.0566],hint:'The Weber fraction is the just noticeable difference divided by the standard weight.',pic:WEBERPT(9)}},
 {skill:'sensation',say:'In vision, light passes through the cornea, pupil and lens and is focused on the retina, where rods work in dim light and cones give color and detail, concentrated in the fovea. Signals leave through the optic nerve, where the blind spot has no receptors, and pass through the thalamus to the occipital lobe. Trichromatic theory explains color at the cones; opponent-process theory explains afterimages. In hearing, the eardrum, ossicles and cochlea pass vibrations to hair cells, which transduce sound; place and frequency theories both help explain pitch. Smell reaches olfactory and limbic areas without the usual thalamic relay, taste has five basic qualities, and the vestibular sense and kinesthesis handle balance and body position. Gate-control theory explains how some input can reduce pain.',eq:'vision: cornea → lens → retina → optic nerve → thalamus → occipital lobe',pic:SENSES},
 {skill:'heredity',summary:true,say:'To work through this unit, first decide what level a question is about. For genes and environment, use concordance and remember that heritability describes groups, not individuals. For communication, separate fast neural signals from slower hormonal ones, and remember that action potentials are all-or-none, with intensity coded by rate. Match each brain region to its job, and choose a scan by whether you need structure or activity. Link brain waves to sleep stages and cycles of about 90 minutes. For sensation, use thresholds and Weber\'s law and follow the path from receptor to cortex. Avoid myths: we do not use only 10 percent of the brain, and people are not strictly left-brained or right-brained.',eq:'choose the level, then the tool, then check the claim against the evidence',pic:WRAP}
],
progression:{requires:[],operations:3,demand:'Separate heredity from environment, follow a message from neuron to cortex, match structures to functions and compute rates, thresholds and cycles from stated data.'},
items(add){/* Round 11 (17 Oct): a pool rounding instruction never asks for more decimals than the stored answer has; "if needed" and significant-figure instructions are kept. Same copy in every unit that uses it (tests/pool-wording-round11.test.js). */const fitRound=(p,a)=>{if(typeof a!=='number'||!Number.isFinite(a)||/if needed/i.test(p))return p;const s=String(a);if(/e/.test(s))return p;const d=(s.split('.')[1]||'').length,W=['zero','one','two','three','four'],NEAR=['whole number','tenth','hundredth','thousandth','ten-thousandth'],m=/(, )?(\b(?:and )?(?:rounded|[Rr]ound) )?to (?:the nearest (tenth|hundredth|thousandth|ten-thousandth)( of a unit)?|(\d|one|two|three|four) (decimal places?|decimals?)\b)/.exec(p);if(!m)return p;const asked=m[3]?NEAR.indexOf(m[3]):/\d/.test(m[5])?+m[5]:W.indexOf(m[5]);if(asked<=d)return p;const head=p.slice(0,m.index),tail=p.slice(m.index+m[0].length);if(d>0)return head+m[0].replace(m[3]||m[5]+' '+m[6],m[3]?NEAR[d]:(/\d/.test(m[5])?d:W[d])+' '+(d===1?'decimal place':m[6]==='decimals'?'decimals':'decimal places'))+tail;const whole=[[/(^|[.?!;] )Give a decimal $/,'$1Give a whole number'],[/(^|[.?!] )Give the number without a percent sign $/,'$1Give a whole number without a percent sign'],[/(^|[.?!] )Give a signed number$/,'$1Give a signed whole number'],[/ as a percent $/,' as a whole-number percent'],[/(^|[.?!] )Give (?:the number|the answer|your answer|it) $/,'$1Give a whole number'],[/(^|[.?!] )(Give [^.?!,]*) $/,'$1$2 as a whole number']];for(const [re,w] of whole)if(re.test(head))return head.replace(re,w)+tail;if(m[2]&&/^[.?!]/.test(tail)&&/(^|[.?!;)] )$/.test(head))return head+(/; $/.test(head)?'give':'Give')+' a whole number'+tail;return head.replace(/ $/,'')+(m[1]?',':'')+(m[2]&&/^and /.test(m[2])?' and give a whole number':' as a whole number'+(m[4]?' of units':''))+tail;};const put0=add;add=(s,p,a,...r)=>put0(s,fitRound(p,a),a,...r);const seen=new Set(TRY);const put=(skill,prompt,answer,pic,explanation,accept)=>{if(seen.has(prompt))return;seen.add(prompt);add(skill,prompt,answer,pic,explanation,accept);};
 for(const [skill,rows] of Object.entries(FACTS))rows.forEach(([prompt,right,w1,w2,explanation],i)=>{const c=choose(right,[w1,w2],i);put(skill,`${prompt} ${c.text}. Answer A, B or C.`,c.letter,scenario('Read the question',prompt),explanation,[right]);});
 for(const [both,one] of [[18,12],[9,21],[5,15],[16,4]])put('heredity',`In a fictional twin study of a trait, ${both} pairs have the trait in both twins and ${one} pairs have it in only one twin. What is the concordance rate, pairs with both twins affected divided by pairs with at least one twin affected? Round to four decimals.`,rnd(both/(both+one)),bars('Fictional twin pairs',['Both affected','One affected'],[both,one]),`Concordance counts pairs with at least one affected twin: ${both} + ${one} = ${both+one}. Divide ${both} by ${both+one}.`);
 for(const [r,t] of [[-72,-55],[-68,-52],[-75,-55]])put('neuron',`A fictional neuron has a resting potential of ${r} mV and a threshold of ${t} mV. By how many millivolts must its membrane potential rise to reach threshold? Round to four decimals.`,t-r,board('Membrane potentials (fictional)',[`resting: ${r} mV`,`threshold: ${t} mV`]),`The rise needed is threshold minus resting: ${t} - (${r}) = ${t-r} millivolts.`);
 for(const [a,b] of [[12,30],[20,35]])put('neuron',`A fictional neuron fires ${a} action potentials per second to a weak stimulus and ${b} per second to a stronger one. By what percent does its firing rate increase? Give the number without a percent sign and round to four decimals.`,rnd((b-a)/a*100),bars('Fictional firing rates (per second)',['Weak','Strong'],[a,b]),`Action potentials keep the same size, so intensity shows in rate. Increase = (${b} - ${a}) ÷ ${a} × 100 = ${rnd((b-a)/a*100)} percent.`);
 for(const h of [6,7,9,10])put('sleep',`A fictional sleeper has sleep cycles that each last exactly 90 minutes. How many full cycles fit into ${h} hours of sleep? Count only complete cycles. Round to four decimals.`,Math.floor(h*60/90),bars('Fictional sleep, in minutes',['Sleep time','One cycle'],[h*60,90]),`${h} hours is ${h*60} minutes. Dividing by 90 gives ${rnd(h*60/90)}, so ${Math.floor(h*60/90)} complete cycles fit.`);
 for(const n of [4,5])put('sleep',`In a fictional model of sleep, every 90-minute cycle includes 20 minutes of REM. How many minutes of REM occur across ${n} complete cycles? Round to four decimals.`,20*n,bars('Fictional minutes per cycle',['REM','Rest of cycle'],[20,70]),`Each cycle has 20 minutes of REM in this model, so ${n} cycles contain ${n} × 20 = ${20*n} minutes. Real REM periods lengthen across the night.`);
 for(const [j,s] of [[6,150],[4,50],[12,120]])put('sensation',`In a fictional experiment, a participant can just detect a change of ${j} g when the standard weight is ${s} g. What is the Weber fraction, the just noticeable difference divided by the standard? Round to four decimals.`,rnd(j/s),WEBERPT(j),`Weber's law uses the proportion JND ÷ standard: ${j} ÷ ${s} = ${rnd(j/s)}. The fraction is fictional.`);
 for(const [k,s] of [[.02,300],[.05,240]])put('sensation',`In a fictional experiment, the Weber fraction for judging line length is ${k}. Using Weber's law, what is the just noticeable difference for a standard line of ${s} mm? Round to four decimals.`,rnd(k*s),board('Weber model supplied',[`fraction k: ${k}`,`standard: ${s} mm`]),`Weber\s law says the JND is a constant proportion of the standard, so JND = k × standard = ${k} × ${s} = ${rnd(k*s)} mm.`);
 for(const [h,m] of [[36,12],[45,5]])put('sensation',`In a fictional signal-detection task, a participant had ${h} hits and ${m} misses on trials when the signal was present. What is the hit rate, hits divided by all signal-present trials? Round to four decimals.`,rnd(h/(h+m)),bars('Fictional signal-present trials',['Hits','Misses'],[h,m]),`Signal-present trials are hits plus misses: ${h} + ${m} = ${h+m}. Hit rate = ${h} ÷ ${h+m} = ${rnd(h/(h+m))}.`);
 for(const [f,c] of [[6,34],[10,40]])put('sensation',`In a fictional signal-detection task, a participant had ${f} false alarms and ${c} correct rejections on trials when no signal was present. What is the false-alarm rate, false alarms divided by all signal-absent trials? Round to four decimals.`,rnd(f/(f+c)),bars('Fictional signal-absent trials',['False alarms','Correct rejections'],[f,c]),`Signal-absent trials are false alarms plus correct rejections: ${f} + ${c} = ${f+c}. The false-alarm rate is ${f} ÷ ${f+c} = ${rnd(f/(f+c))}.`);
}
};
const FACTS={
heredity:[
 ["A psychologist explains that a child's personality is shaped by genes and experiences acting together and influencing each other. Which view is this?","an interaction of nature and nurture","nature alone, with no role for experience","nurture alone, with no role for genes","Most psychologists reject a pure nature or pure nurture view: genes and environment work together, and experiences can even change how genes are expressed."],
 ["A news story says a trait is \"60% heritable,\" so 60% of Maria's trait comes from her genes. What is the error in the claim?","heritability describes variation within a group, not how much of one person's trait is genetic","heritability can only be calculated for physical traits, never for behavior","heritability is always 100% for traits that appear in families","Heritability is a statistic about differences among people in a particular population and environment. It cannot divide up one individual's trait into genetic and environmental parts."],
 ["Why are identical (monozygotic) twins especially informative for behavior geneticists?","they share nearly all of their segregating genes","they always grow up in different environments","they have completely different genes","Monozygotic twins come from one fertilized egg and share nearly all of their genes, so comparing them with fraternal twins helps researchers estimate genetic influence."],
 ["On average, fraternal (dizygotic) twins share about what fraction of the genes that vary among people?","one half","all of them","one quarter","Dizygotic twins develop from two eggs and are as genetically similar as ordinary siblings, sharing about half of the genes that differ among people on average."],
 ["In a fictional adoption study, adopted children's scores on a trait correlate more strongly with their biological parents' scores than with their adoptive parents' scores. Which interpretation is best supported?","genetic differences probably contribute to the trait","the shared home environment alone must explain the trait","the trait has no environmental influence at all","Resemblance to biological parents despite a different home suggests a genetic contribution, but it does not rule out environmental effects, so the conclusion should be hedged."],
 ["How can epigenetic changes affect behavior?","by altering gene expression without changing the DNA base sequence","by rewriting the DNA sequence in every body cell","by deleting genes the person inherited","Epigenetic marks, such as chemical tags on DNA or its packaging, can turn genes up or down in activity while the underlying DNA sequence stays the same."],
 ["A fictional study finds that a gene variant raises the risk of anxiety only in people who also experienced high stress. Which concept does this illustrate?","a gene-environment interaction","genetic determinism","a purely environmental effect","The effect of the gene depends on the environment, which is what an interaction means. Genes do not act alone, and the environment's effect can depend on genes."],
 ["Which question would a psychologist using the evolutionary perspective most likely ask?","how a behavior may have helped ancestors survive and reproduce","how unconscious childhood conflicts shape adult behavior","how reinforcement strengthens a learned response","Evolutionary psychology looks at how behavioral and mental traits may have been shaped by natural selection over many generations. Its explanations are hypotheses that must be tested."],
 ["Traits that help organisms survive and reproduce in their environment tend to become more common in a population over generations. What is this process called?","natural selection","neuroplasticity","operant conditioning","Natural selection acts on heritable variation: traits linked to more offspring tend to spread. It operates on populations over generations, not on one person's lifetime."],
 ["Which term refers to an organism's observable characteristics, produced by its genes together with its environment?","phenotype","genotype","chromosome","Genotype is the genetic makeup; phenotype is what can be observed, such as height or behavior, and it results from genes and environment together."],
 ["What does the field of behavior genetics study?","how genes and environment contribute to differences in behavior and traits","how a brain region is removed to see what behavior changes","how people think about their own thinking","Behavior genetics uses tools such as twin and adoption studies to estimate how much genetic and environmental differences relate to differences among people."],
 ["Identical twins raised apart are similar in a fictional trait. A critic points out that adoption agencies often placed them in similar homes. What concern does the critic raise?","similar environments could be a confounding explanation for the similarity","identical twins are not genetically similar","similarity can never be evidence of anything","If the \"separate\" homes were alike, the similarity could come from shared environment rather than shared genes, so the study cannot cleanly separate the two."],
 ["Humans typically have how many chromosomes in each body cell (not counting egg or sperm cells)?","46, arranged in 23 pairs","23, one single set","48, arranged in 24 pairs","Each typical human body cell has 23 pairs of chromosomes, 46 in all: one of each pair from each parent. Egg and sperm cells carry 23."],
 ["A gene is best described as what?","a segment of DNA that carries instructions for making a product such as a protein","an entire chromosome with all of its genes","a behavior that is passed down in families","Genes are stretches of DNA; chromosomes are long DNA molecules holding many genes. Genes influence behavior only through long chains of biological and environmental events."],
 ["Many psychological traits are described as polygenic. What does that mean?","many genes each contribute a small effect","one single gene fully determines the trait","only the environment influences the trait","Most complex traits, including those related to behavior, are influenced by many genes with small effects interacting with the environment, not by a single gene."],
 ["Within well-nourished groups, height is highly heritable, yet average height in many countries has risen over the past century. What does this show?","high heritability within a group does not make a trait fixed against environmental change","height is not influenced by genes at all","heritability measures how fast a trait changes over time","Heritability describes differences within a group at one time. Changes in nutrition and health can shift a whole population's average even when heritability is high."],
 ["In a twin study, identical twins show a higher concordance rate than fraternal twins for a trait. What does this suggest?","a genetic contribution to the trait","that genes play no role","that fraternal twins have identical genes","If the more genetically similar pair agree more often, genes likely contribute. It does not show the trait is entirely genetic, since twins also share environments."]
],
nervous:[
 ["Which two structures make up the central nervous system?","the brain and the spinal cord","the sympathetic and parasympathetic systems","the somatic and autonomic systems","The central nervous system (CNS) is the brain and spinal cord. Everything else is the peripheral nervous system."],
 ["Which division of the nervous system carries information between the central nervous system and the rest of the body?","the peripheral nervous system","the central nervous system","the endocrine system","The peripheral nervous system (PNS) consists of nerves that link the CNS to sensory receptors, muscles and glands."],
 ["Which part of the peripheral nervous system controls voluntary movements of skeletal muscles?","the somatic nervous system","the autonomic nervous system","the parasympathetic nervous system","The somatic nervous system carries sensory input and conveys commands to skeletal muscles. The autonomic system handles largely automatic functions."],
 ["Heart rate, digestion and gland activity are mainly regulated by which part of the nervous system?","the autonomic nervous system","the somatic nervous system","the association areas of the cortex","The autonomic nervous system controls internal organs and glands, usually without conscious effort, through its sympathetic and parasympathetic branches."],
 ["A hiker sees a bear. Her heart rate increases, her pupils dilate and digestion slows. Which branch is mainly responsible?","the sympathetic nervous system","the parasympathetic nervous system","the somatic nervous system","The sympathetic branch arouses the body for action, the \"fight-or-flight\" response, by raising heart rate and diverting energy from digestion."],
 ["After the bear leaves, the hiker's heart rate gradually slows and digestion resumes. Which branch is mainly responsible?","the parasympathetic nervous system","the sympathetic nervous system","the somatic nervous system","The parasympathetic branch calms the body and conserves energy, often called \"rest and digest.\" The two branches work together to keep the body balanced."],
 ["In a spinal reflex such as pulling a hand from a hot pan, which type of neuron connects sensory neurons to motor neurons within the spinal cord?","interneurons","glial cells","sensory neurons","Interneurons lie within the CNS and link incoming sensory neurons to outgoing motor neurons. In a simple reflex the loop can act before the brain registers pain."],
 ["Which neurons carry messages from sensory receptors toward the central nervous system?","sensory (afferent) neurons","motor (efferent) neurons","interneurons only","Sensory (afferent) neurons carry information in; motor (efferent) neurons carry commands out; interneurons link them inside the CNS."],
 ["Motor (efferent) neurons carry messages from the central nervous system to which structures?","muscles and glands","sensory receptors","other sensory neurons only","Motor neurons deliver commands to effectors, which are muscles and glands. Their name comes from motor movement, though glands are targets too."],
 ["Which system communicates mainly by releasing chemical messengers called hormones into the bloodstream?","the endocrine system","the somatic nervous system","the reflex arc","The endocrine system uses glands that secrete hormones into the blood, where they reach distant target cells that have matching receptors."],
 ["Compared with messages sent by neurons, hormonal messages generally do which of the following?","travel more slowly but often have longer-lasting effects","travel faster and last only milliseconds","have no effect on behavior","Neural signals can act within milliseconds, while hormones diffuse through the blood and may take seconds to minutes or longer and may act for a longer time."],
 ["Which gland is often called the \"master gland\" because its hormones influence other glands?","the pituitary gland","the pancreas","the thyroid gland","The pituitary releases hormones that regulate other endocrine glands, and it is itself influenced by the hypothalamus."],
 ["Which hormone, released by the adrenal glands, boosts heart rate and available energy during a stress response?","epinephrine (adrenaline)","melatonin","insulin","The adrenal glands release epinephrine and norepinephrine, which help prepare the body for action. Melatonin affects sleep and insulin affects blood sugar."],
 ["Which gland releases insulin and glucagon, which regulate blood sugar?","the pancreas","the pituitary gland","the adrenal gland","The pancreas contains cells that secrete insulin and glucagon. Their balance helps keep blood glucose stable."],
 ["Which hormone, released by the pineal gland mainly in darkness, helps regulate the sleep-wake cycle?","melatonin","oxytocin","cortisol","The pineal gland secretes melatonin in a rhythm tied to light and dark. Oxytocin is released by the pituitary; cortisol comes from the adrenal glands."],
 ["What is a main role of glial cells in the nervous system?","supporting neurons by giving structure, insulation and nutrients","replacing neurons as the cells that fire action potentials","releasing hormones into the bloodstream","Glial cells are roughly as numerous as neurons in the brain and support them in several ways, including forming myelin and clearing waste. Neurons carry most rapid signaling."],
 ["Which statement correctly compares the nervous and endocrine systems?","both use chemical messengers, but the nervous system's signals are usually faster","only the endocrine system uses chemical messengers","the endocrine system is usually faster than the nervous system","Neurons release neurotransmitters across synapses while glands release hormones into blood. Both are chemical signals, but nerve impulses usually act more quickly."],
 ["Which pairing best describes the body's rapid stress response?","sympathetic activation with adrenal hormone release","parasympathetic activation with melatonin release","somatic activation with insulin release","Stress activates the sympathetic branch, which also prompts the adrenal glands to secrete epinephrine, linking the nervous and endocrine systems."]
],
neuron:[
 ["Which part of a neuron mainly receives incoming signals from other neurons?","the dendrites","the axon terminals","the myelin sheath","Dendrites are branching extensions that receive input. The axon carries signals away and the terminals release neurotransmitters."],
 ["What is the main job of a neuron's axon?","carrying the action potential away from the cell body","receiving neurotransmitters from other neurons","making hormones for the bloodstream","The axon is the long extension that conducts the action potential toward the terminal buttons, where neurotransmitter is released."],
 ["What is a main function of the myelin sheath that insulates many axons?","it speeds the conduction of the signal","it slows the signal to prevent errors","it makes neurotransmitters","Myelin, formed by glial cells, speeds action potentials by making them jump between gaps called nodes of Ranvier."],
 ["In a fictional patient with a disease that damages myelin, such as multiple sclerosis, what effect on neural signaling is expected?","slowed or disrupted signal conduction","faster than normal conduction","no change in conduction","Damage to myelin interferes with the speed and reliability of conduction, which helps explain symptoms such as weakness and numbness."],
 ["What is the term for a neuron's stable state when it is not firing, with the inside of the cell more negative than the outside?","the resting potential","the action potential","the refractory period","The resting potential is about -70 millivolts in many textbook examples, and it is maintained by ion concentrations and the membrane's selective permeability."],
 ["Under what condition does a neuron generate an action potential?","when the combined input reaches the threshold","when the membrane is hyperpolarized below the resting level","whenever any single input arrives, however weak","The neuron integrates excitatory and inhibitory input. If the total reaches the threshold, an action potential fires."],
 ["What does the all-or-none principle state about neurons?","a neuron either fires a full action potential or does not fire","stronger stimuli produce larger action potentials","partial action potentials scale with the stimulus","The size of an action potential does not vary with stimulus strength. Strength is coded by how often neurons fire and how many neurons fire."],
 ["How does the nervous system mainly signal a stronger stimulus?","by firing more often and recruiting more neurons","by producing taller action potentials","by lowering the threshold to zero","Because action potentials are all-or-none, intensity is coded by firing rate and by the number of neurons activated."],
 ["What is true of a neuron during its refractory period?","it is temporarily unable or less able to fire another action potential","it fires continuously","it is permanently damaged","After firing, the neuron briefly resets, which limits the maximum firing rate and helps the impulse travel in one direction."],
 ["Which ion movement is most important in the rising (depolarization) phase of an action potential?","sodium ions flowing into the axon","potassium ions flowing into the axon","sodium ions flowing out of the axon","Voltage-gated sodium channels open at threshold, letting positive sodium ions enter and make the inside less negative. Potassium leaving later helps restore the negative resting state."],
 ["What is the tiny gap between the axon terminal of one neuron and the next cell called?","the synaptic cleft","the node of Ranvier","the axon hillock","The synapse is the junction; the cleft is the small space across which neurotransmitter molecules diffuse."],
 ["From which structures are neurotransmitters released into the synapse?","vesicles in the presynaptic neuron's axon terminals","the dendrites of the presynaptic neuron","the myelin sheath","When an action potential reaches the terminal, vesicles fuse with the membrane and release neurotransmitter into the cleft."],
 ["What is reuptake?","the presynaptic neuron reabsorbs neurotransmitter from the synaptic cleft","the postsynaptic neuron produces new neurotransmitter","myelin breaks down after a signal","Reuptake clears the synapse and recycles the transmitter, ending its signal. Many drugs act by blocking it."],
 ["GABA is the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter. What is its general effect on the postsynaptic neuron?","it makes firing less likely","it makes firing more likely","it controls voluntary movement directly","Inhibitory messages push the postsynaptic neuron away from threshold, balancing excitatory input such as glutamate."],
 ["The loss of dopamine-producing neurons in a movement-related brain area is associated with which condition?","Parkinson's disease","multiple sclerosis","narcolepsy","Parkinson's disease involves loss of dopamine neurons and movement problems. Dopamine also plays roles in reward and motivation."],
 ["Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) act in which way?","they block reuptake of serotonin so more stays in the synapse","they speed the removal of serotonin from the synapse","they destroy serotonin-producing neurons","By blocking the reuptake pump, SSRIs raise the amount of serotonin available in the cleft. Their full therapeutic effects develop over time and are not fully explained by this alone."],
 ["A drug that mimics a neurotransmitter and activates its receptors is called what?","an agonist","an antagonist","a hormone","Agonists bind to receptors and produce an effect similar to the natural transmitter. Opioid drugs act as agonists at receptors that normally respond to endorphins."],
 ["A drug that attaches to a receptor and blocks the neurotransmitter from binding to it is called what?","an antagonist","an agonist","a stimulant","Antagonists occupy receptors without activating them, reducing the neurotransmitter's effect."],
 ["Alcohol, barbiturates and benzodiazepines are classified as which type of psychoactive drug?","depressants","stimulants","hallucinogens","Depressants slow neural activity and bodily functions. Alcohol, for example, affects GABA and glutamate systems, among others."],
 ["Cocaine increases the amount of dopamine in synapses mainly by what mechanism?","blocking dopamine reuptake","destroying dopamine neurons","mimicking GABA at its receptors","Cocaine blocks the transporters that remove dopamine from the cleft, prolonging its action in reward pathways."],
 ["After repeated use, a person needs larger doses of a drug to get the same effect. What is this called?","tolerance","withdrawal","reuptake","Tolerance is a reduced response with repeated exposure. Withdrawal is the discomfort that follows when use is reduced or stopped."],
 ["Morphine and heroin relieve pain mainly by acting on which system?","opioid receptors, mimicking endorphins","dopamine reuptake pumps","myelin formation","Opioids bind to receptors for the body's own endorphins, reducing pain signals and producing pleasurable feelings."]
],
brain:[
 ["Which brainstem structure helps control heartbeat and breathing?","the medulla","the cerebellum","the hippocampus","The medulla, at the base of the brain, regulates vital automatic functions. Severe damage there can be life-threatening."],
 ["Damage to which structure would most likely cause jerky, poorly coordinated movement and balance problems?","the cerebellum","the thalamus","the amygdala","The cerebellum helps coordinate voluntary movement and balance and supports some forms of learning and timing."],
 ["Which network extending through the brainstem helps regulate arousal and alertness?","the reticular formation","the corpus callosum","the hippocampus","The reticular formation filters stimuli and affects sleep, wakefulness and attention."],
 ["Which forebrain structure acts as a relay station for most sensory information on its way to the cortex?","the thalamus","the hypothalamus","the medulla","The thalamus routes most sensory signals to the cortex; smell is an exception because it reaches olfactory regions more directly."],
 ["Which structure regulates hunger, thirst and body temperature and also directs the pituitary gland?","the hypothalamus","the thalamus","the pons","The hypothalamus lies below the thalamus and links the nervous system to the endocrine system through the pituitary."],
 ["Which limbic structure is most strongly associated with processing fear and other strong emotions?","the amygdala","the hippocampus","the cerebellum","The amygdala is involved in emotional learning and responses. Emotion involves many brain regions, not the amygdala alone."],
 ["Which limbic structure is most important for forming new explicit (conscious) memories?","the hippocampus","the amygdala","the medulla","The hippocampus helps consolidate new facts and events. Damage can leave a person unable to form new lasting explicit memories."],
 ["Which group of structures is most associated with the limbic system?","amygdala, hippocampus and hypothalamus","medulla, pons and cerebellum","frontal, parietal and occipital lobes","The limbic system is a set of interconnected structures involved in emotion, memory and motivation."],
 ["Which lobe of the cerebral cortex is most involved in planning, judgment and impulse control?","the frontal lobe","the occipital lobe","the temporal lobe","The frontal lobes, especially the prefrontal cortex, support executive functions such as planning and self-control."],
 ["Phineas Gage survived an accident in which an iron rod passed through his frontal lobe, and his personality was reported to change. What is the case most often used to illustrate?","damage to frontal areas can alter personality and decision making","the cerebellum controls personality","the brain cannot be injured","Gage's case suggested that frontal regions contribute to personality and social behavior, although reports of his change were partly anecdotal and are debated."],
 ["Touch, pressure and pain sensations from the body are processed mainly in the somatosensory cortex, located in which lobe?","the parietal lobe","the temporal lobe","the frontal lobe","The somatosensory cortex lies in the front of the parietal lobes, just behind the motor cortex."],
 ["Which lobe contains the primary auditory cortex?","the temporal lobe","the occipital lobe","the parietal lobe","The temporal lobes, near the ears, process sound and contribute to language comprehension and memory."],
 ["Which lobe contains the primary visual cortex?","the occipital lobe","the frontal lobe","the temporal lobe","The occipital lobe at the back of the brain receives visual information relayed from the eyes through the thalamus."],
 ["Which area, at the rear of the frontal lobe, sends signals that control voluntary movement?","the motor cortex","the somatosensory cortex","the visual cortex","The motor cortex sends commands to muscles; the somatosensory cortex receives touch information."],
 ["A fictional patient understands speech fairly well but speaks slowly with great effort. Damage to which area is most likely?","Broca's area","Wernicke's area","the visual cortex","Broca's area, usually in the left frontal lobe, is important for producing speech. Damage can cause Broca's aphasia."],
 ["A fictional patient speaks fluently but with words that do not make sense and has trouble understanding others. Damage to which area is most likely?","Wernicke's area","Broca's area","the somatosensory cortex","Wernicke's area, usually in the left temporal lobe, is important for language comprehension. Damage can cause Wernicke's aphasia."],
 ["What are the association areas of the cerebral cortex?","regions that integrate information from sensory, motor and memory processes","regions that only control reflexes","regions that contain no neurons","Association areas are not primarily sensory or motor; they integrate information and support thinking, planning and language."],
 ["Which structure is a large band of axons connecting the brain's two hemispheres?","the corpus callosum","the thalamus","the reticular formation","The corpus callosum lets the hemispheres share information. Cutting it is a rare treatment for severe epilepsy."],
 ["In a split-brain patient, an object shown only in the right visual field can usually be named aloud. Why?","the information goes to the left hemisphere, which usually supports speech","the right hemisphere usually controls speech","the corpus callosum carries the information after surgery","Each visual field projects to the opposite hemisphere. For most people language is mostly left-lateralized, so right-field input can be named."],
 ["A student claims people are either \"left-brained\" (logical) or \"right-brained\" (creative). Which response is best supported by research?","most complex tasks use both hemispheres working together","each person relies on only one hemisphere for all tasks","the hemispheres never communicate","Some functions are lateralized, such as language in most people, but neuroimaging does not support strict left-brained or right-brained types."],
 ["A stroke patient regains some lost function as nearby brain areas take over part of the job. Which concept does this illustrate?","neuroplasticity","the all-or-none principle","reuptake","Neuroplasticity is the brain's ability to change its connections and organization with experience or after injury, though recovery is limited."],
 ["Which technique records the brain's electrical activity using electrodes on the scalp?","EEG","MRI","CT scan","An EEG measures summed electrical activity with good timing precision but limited spatial detail."],
 ["Which technique shows which brain areas are active by tracking changes in blood oxygen levels during a task?","fMRI","EEG","CT scan","Functional MRI detects changes in blood oxygenation related to neural activity and shows function as well as structure."],
 ["Which technique uses a radioactive tracer to show where the brain is using energy such as glucose?","PET scan","MRI","EEG","A PET scan tracks tracer molecules to map metabolic activity. It involves exposure to a small radioactive dose."],
 ["Which technique uses magnetic fields and radio waves to produce detailed images of brain structure?","MRI","PET scan","EEG","Structural MRI gives detailed pictures of soft tissue; a CT scan uses X-rays and gives less detail of soft tissue."]
],
sleep:[
 ["What is the term for the body's roughly 24-hour biological rhythm?","circadian rhythm","REM rebound","sleep spindle","Circadian rhythms are daily cycles in alertness, body temperature and hormones, influenced by light."],
 ["Which structure in the hypothalamus acts as the brain's master circadian clock?","the suprachiasmatic nucleus","the reticular formation","the amygdala","The suprachiasmatic nucleus receives light information from the eyes and helps keep the body's rhythms in step with the day."],
 ["Bright light late at night can affect sleep in which way?","it can suppress melatonin and delay sleepiness","it increases melatonin and speeds sleep onset","it has no effect on circadian rhythm","Light, especially bright light in the evening, can reduce melatonin release and shift the body clock later in some people."],
 ["Which stage of sleep is characterized by sleep spindles and K-complexes in the EEG?","NREM stage 2","REM sleep","NREM stage 3","Stage 2 is a light stage of sleep where brief bursts called spindles and K-complexes appear, and it takes up a large share of the night."],
 ["Large, slow delta waves are the main feature of which stage?","NREM stage 3","NREM stage 1","REM sleep","Stage 3 is deep slow-wave sleep, when it is hardest to wake a person and body repair processes are thought to be important."],
 ["Which brain wave pattern is typical of a relaxed, drowsy person who is awake with eyes closed?","alpha waves","delta waves","beta waves","Alpha waves occur in relaxed wakefulness. Beta waves accompany alert thinking and delta waves occur in deep sleep."],
 ["Which sleep stage involves rapid eye movements, vivid dreaming, muscle paralysis and brain activity resembling wakefulness?","REM sleep","NREM stage 3","NREM stage 2","REM sleep is sometimes called paradoxical sleep because the brain is active while major muscles are largely inactive."],
 ["After several nights of REM deprivation, people tend to spend more time in REM when allowed to sleep normally. What is this called?","REM rebound","circadian drift","sleep spindling","REM rebound suggests that the body has a need for REM sleep."],
 ["How do sleep stages typically change across a night?","deep NREM stage 3 occurs mostly early and REM periods lengthen later","stage 3 occurs mostly late and REM periods shorten","each stage occurs once in a fixed order","Cycles last about 90 minutes in adults; early cycles have more slow-wave sleep and later cycles have longer REM periods."],
 ["Which sleep disorder involves repeated pauses in breathing during sleep and is often associated with loud snoring and daytime sleepiness?","sleep apnea","narcolepsy","insomnia","Sleep apnea disrupts sleep repeatedly, even though the person may not remember waking."],
 ["Which disorder involves sudden attacks of overwhelming sleepiness during the day and can include a rapid entry into REM sleep?","narcolepsy","insomnia","sleep apnea","Narcolepsy involves REM-related features, such as sudden muscle weakness, intruding into waking hours."],
 ["Which sleep problem is defined by persistent trouble falling asleep, staying asleep or getting restful sleep?","insomnia","narcolepsy","sleep apnea","Insomnia is a common sleep problem that can be related to stress, habits, health conditions and other causes."],
 ["In children, night terrors typically occur during which part of sleep?","NREM stage 3","REM sleep","NREM stage 1","Night terrors involve apparent panic with little memory afterward and usually occur in deep NREM sleep, unlike nightmares, which occur in REM."],
 ["Which theory proposes that dreams result from the brain's attempt to make sense of random neural activity during REM sleep?","activation-synthesis theory","Freud's wish-fulfillment theory","the opponent-process theory","Activation-synthesis theory proposes that the cortex builds a story from internally generated signals. Many other accounts of dreaming exist."],
 ["In Freud's dream theory, what is the remembered storyline of a dream called?","manifest content","latent content","REM rebound","Freud proposed that the manifest content hides latent content, the underlying symbolic meaning. His interpretations have limited scientific support."],
 ["Which function of sleep is supported by research on learning?","sleep helps consolidate new memories","sleep erases all memories of the day","sleep has no known functions","Studies link sleep, including both slow-wave and REM stages, with strengthening memories, though the details are still being studied."],
 ["A fictional student regularly gets far less sleep than needed. Which outcome is most consistent with research on sleep deprivation?","poorer attention and mood","sharper attention and memory","no change in performance","Sleep deprivation is associated with impaired attention, memory and mood and with health risks."]
],
sensation:[
 ["What is transduction in sensation?","converting physical energy into neural signals","organizing sensations into meaningful perceptions","ignoring a constant stimulus","Sensory receptors transduce light, sound waves, pressure or chemicals into neural impulses the brain can process."],
 ["Which statement distinguishes sensation from perception?","sensation detects stimuli; perception organizes and interprets them","sensation interprets stimuli; perception detects them","they are the same process","Sensation is the detection and basic coding of stimulus energy. Perception is the brain's organization and interpretation of those signals."],
 ["What is the term for the minimum stimulation needed to detect a particular stimulus 50 percent of the time?","absolute threshold","difference threshold","sensory adaptation","The absolute threshold is a statistical point, not a fixed line, because detection varies with a person's state and the situation."],
 ["What is the term for the minimum difference between two stimuli that a person can detect 50 percent of the time?","difference threshold (just noticeable difference)","absolute threshold","transduction","The difference threshold, or just noticeable difference (JND), depends on the intensity of the original stimulus."],
 ["According to Weber's law, the just noticeable difference for a stimulus is what?","a constant proportion of the original stimulus intensity","the same fixed amount at every intensity","smaller when the stimulus is heavier","Weber's law says the change needed to be noticed grows with the size of the original stimulus; the proportion is the Weber fraction. It is an approximation that fits best over middle ranges."],
 ["After a few minutes, a person stops noticing the feel of a watch on the wrist. What is this called?","sensory adaptation","transduction","an absolute threshold","Sensory adaptation is diminished sensitivity to constant stimulation, which lets us pay attention to changes."],
 ["In a signal detection task, a participant says \"yes\" when no signal was presented. What is this response called?","a false alarm","a miss","a hit","Signal detection theory separates a person's sensitivity from their response bias; saying yes when no stimulus is present is a false alarm."],
 ["Which photoreceptors work best in dim light and do not detect color?","rods","cones","bipolar cells","Rods are highly sensitive and located mostly in the periphery of the retina; cones handle color and detail in brighter light."],
 ["Where in the retina are cones most densely concentrated, giving the sharpest detail and color vision?","the fovea","the blind spot","the peripheral retina","The fovea lies at the center of the retina and is rich in cones. Objects are sharpest when their images fall there."],
 ["Why is there a blind spot in each eye?","the optic nerve leaves the eye there, so there are no receptors","the lens cannot focus light there","rods and cones die with age","Where the axons of ganglion cells gather to exit the eye, there are no rods or cones. The brain normally fills in the gap."],
 ["What does trichromatic theory propose about color vision?","three types of cones respond to different ranges of wavelengths","color depends only on rod activity","color depends on a single type of cone","Cones are roughly sensitive to short, medium and long wavelengths, and combined activity codes color."],
 ["After staring at a green square, a viewer sees a reddish afterimage on a white wall. Which theory best explains this?","opponent-process theory","place theory","gate-control theory","Opponent-process theory proposes red-green, blue-yellow and black-white pairs; fatigue of one member produces its opposite afterimage."],
 ["Which sequence correctly orders the path of light into the eye up to the retina?","cornea, pupil, lens, retina","retina, lens, pupil, cornea","lens, cornea, retina, pupil","Light passes through the cornea, then the pupil, which the iris sizes, then the lens, which focuses it onto the retina."],
 ["After leaving the eye through the optic nerve, visual information is typically relayed through which structure on the way to the visual cortex?","the thalamus","the cerebellum","the hippocampus","The thalamus relays most sensory input to the cortex. Within the visual cortex, feature detectors respond to edges, motion and other features."],
 ["Which sequence correctly orders the parts of the ear that pass sound vibrations inward?","eardrum, ossicles, cochlea","cochlea, ossicles, eardrum","ossicles, eardrum, cochlea","Sound waves make the eardrum vibrate, the three ossicles amplify the vibrations, and the cochlea's fluid carries them to the receptor cells."],
 ["Where does auditory transduction occur?","at hair cells in the cochlea","at the pinna","at the eardrum","Vibrations bend hair cells on the basilar membrane, which convert movement to neural signals sent along the auditory nerve."],
 ["Which theory proposes that the rate of neural firing matches a sound's frequency, helping to explain low-pitched sounds?","frequency theory","place theory","opponent-process theory","Frequency theory can account for low pitches, while place theory, based on where on the basilar membrane the wave peaks, better explains high pitches. Both contribute."],
 ["Damage to the hair cells or the auditory nerve most directly causes which kind of hearing loss?","sensorineural hearing loss","conduction hearing loss","sensory adaptation","Conduction loss involves the middle-ear mechanics; sensorineural loss involves the receptor cells or nerve and is often permanent."],
 ["Smell is unusual among the senses because its signals do what?","reach olfactory and limbic areas without the usual thalamic relay","must pass through the occipital lobe first","are processed only by the cerebellum","Olfactory signals travel from the nose to the olfactory bulb and then to areas such as the olfactory cortex and limbic structures, which may help explain strong links between smell and emotion."],
 ["Which of the following is considered one of the five basic tastes?","umami","spicy","metallic","The basic tastes usually listed are sweet, sour, salty, bitter and umami. Spiciness is mostly pain and heat sensation."],
 ["Rubbing a bumped elbow can reduce pain. Which theory explains this?","gate-control theory","trichromatic theory","frequency theory","Gate-control theory proposes that non-painful input can close a \"gate\" in the spinal cord to reduce pain messages, though pain is also shaped by the brain and mood."],
 ["Which sense of balance and head position depends on the semicircular canals?","the vestibular sense","kinesthesis","gustation","The vestibular system in the inner ear detects head movement and position. Kinesthesis senses the position and movement of body parts."],
 ["Which sense tells you the position and movement of your body parts?","kinesthesis","vestibular sense","olfaction","Receptors in muscles, tendons and joints support kinesthesis. It lets you touch your nose with your eyes closed."]
]
};
